It's three weeks since the release of xfs-0.6.0 and two weeks since the
release of xfs-0.6.1. More than a dozen of people contacted me, either
reported their success or the bug they found. Mika even banchmarked it.
So far, no single bug was found in the kernel part. Five bugs was found
in xfsck and mkxfs, 4 were silly bugs and one was not that silly.
All bugs are fixed except a harmless off-1 in xfs-0.6.1 release. 
I am thinking about turn it into BETA (version 0.7) if no new bugs are
found in a week or so. For those who have tried xfs, please report
your test, by send email to me, so I can judge if it's stable enough to
call it BETA.

For your reference, xfs is a file system designed and implemented for 
Linux, which retains the stability of minix fs and eliminates the constrains
the minix fs has. xfs keeps all the info a "real" unix fs has, such  as
separate ctime/mtime/atime. xfs uses 32-bit zone pointer, which can 
potentially support 4TB volumn size (currently 4GB) and 4GB file size
(wait for kernel support, currently 64MB for a single file). xfs support
long file names. I have no interest to include every fancy features, 
but features a light multi-user system or a server on a small LAN will 
need for general purpose. Basically neither accounting nor ACL will be
supported. 

